 Certainly! Here's a JavaScript function that should help you swap the positions of the first and second adjectives (if there are exactly two adjectives) in a given sentence:

```javascript
function switchAdjectiveOrder(sentence) {
  const regex = /(?:^|\s)([a-z]+(?=\s|[,.?!;])|[a-z]+\s+(?:a|an|the)\s+([a-z]+))(\s+([a-z]+(?:\.|[,?!;]))|$)/g; // RegExp for matching adjective phrases
  let match;
  let newSentence = sentence;

  while ((match = regex.exec(sentence)) !== null) {
    // Swap the order of adjectives in the matched phrase
    [match[1], match[3]] = [match[3], match[1]];

    // Update the sentence string with the modified phrase
    const index = match.index;
    newSentence =
      newSentence.slice(0, index) +
      match[3] +
      ' ' +
      match[1] +
      newSentence.slice(match.index + match[0].length);
  }

  return newSentence;
}
```

You can use this function by passing a sentence as an argument, like so:

```javascript
const input = "Beautiful green trees are in the park";
const output = switchAdjectiveOrder(input);
console.log(output); // "Green beautiful trees are in the park"
```

This function uses a regular expression to search for adjective phrases in the sentence, which includes a determiner (e.g., "a", "an", "the") between the adjective and the noun. It then swaps the order of the first and third elements in each matched phrase and updates the sentence accordingly.

